sections.s2_technical:
In a plain REST API, the client must know every URL in advance, hardcoded from documentation. HATEOAS flips this: the server returns links in the response, and the client follows them. This is the uniform interface constraint of REST taken to its fullest, where hypermedia drives the interaction.

A link has at least a relation name, called a rel, and a URL, called an href. The rel tells the client the meaning, like self, transactions, or freeze, and the href is where to go. The client branches on the rel it understands, not on a memorised URL string.

```json
{
  "accountId": "SAV2001",
  "balance": 1000000.00,
  "status": "ACTIVE",
  "_links": {
    "self":         { "href": "/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001" },
    "transactions": { "href": "/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/transactions" },
    "transfer":     { "href": "/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/transfers" },
    "freeze":       { "href": "/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/freeze" }
  }
}
```

The crucial idea is that links are state-driven. The server includes only the links that are valid for the resource's current state. An active account includes freeze and transfer; a frozen account includes unfreeze and omits transfer; a closed account includes neither. The client does not need to know the business rules about which actions are allowed; it simply renders the links present in the response. This decouples the client from the server's state machine.

Spring HATEOAS provides the building blocks. RepresentationModel is a base class that holds links. EntityModel wraps a DTO and adds links. The linkTo and methodOn helpers build links from controller methods in a type-safe way, so if you rename a path the link updates.

```java
import static org.springframework.hateoas.server.mvc.WebMvcLinkBuilder.*;
import org.springframework.hateoas.EntityModel;

@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
public EntityModel<AccountDto> get(@PathVariable String id) {
    AccountDto dto = service.get(id);
    EntityModel<AccountDto> model = EntityModel.of(dto);
    model.add(linkTo(methodOn(AccountController.class).get(id)).withSelfRel());
    model.add(linkTo(methodOn(AccountController.class).transactions(id)).withRel("transactions"));
    if (dto.isActive()) {
        model.add(linkTo(methodOn(AccountController.class).freeze(id)).withRel("freeze"));
    } else {
        model.add(linkTo(methodOn(AccountController.class).unfreeze(id)).withRel("unfreeze"));
    }
    return model;
}
```

The role of HATEOAS is to make the API self-descriptive and evolvable. Because clients follow links, the server can change URLs without breaking clients that follow rels, and clients can adapt to available actions without hardcoding business rules. It also improves discoverability, since a developer exploring the API sees what is possible from each response.

The common media types for hypermedia are HAL, with its underscore links structure, which Spring HATEOAS uses by default, and others like Collection+JSON or the richer HAL-FORMS that can also describe the method and input fields for each action.

A senior nuance: full HATEOAS is powerful but adds complexity, and many teams adopt it partially, including links for navigation and state-dependent actions while clients still know base URLs. The highest value in a banking API is the state-driven actions, because the client no longer has to replicate the server's rules about when an account can be frozen or a transfer allowed. The honest trade-off is that few clients fully drive themselves from hypermedia, so I apply HATEOAS where state-dependent discoverability genuinely helps rather than as dogma everywhere.

